   168	\subsection{Statistical Methodology}
   169	
   170	Following Colas et al.~\cite{colas2019hitchhiker}, we report:
   171	\begin{itemize}
   172	    \item Mean $\pm$ standard error across seeds
   173	    \item 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals (10,000 resamples)
   174	    \item Welch's t-test for pairwise algorithm comparisons
   175	    \item \texttt{rliable}~\cite{agarwal2021deep} aggregate metrics (IQM, median, optimality gap)
   176	\end{itemize}
   177	
   178	All pairwise comparisons were evaluated using Welch's independent-samples $t$-test
   179	(unequal variances assumed) and the non-parametric Mann--Whitney $U$ test as a
   180	robustness check. Effect sizes are reported as Cohen's $d$ with the pooled
   181	standard deviation estimator; confidence intervals follow the
   182	Hedges--Olkin (1985) analytical formula and are reported at the 95\% level
   183	(two-tailed, $z = 1.96$). Post-hoc statistical power was computed under the
   184	observed effect size and sample sizes at $\alpha = 0.05$.
   185	
   186	To address the multiple-comparisons problem across 5 planned tests,
   187	we apply both the conservative Bonferroni correction (family-wise error rate:
   188	$\alpha' = \alpha / k = 0.0100$) and the Benjamini--Hochberg procedure
   189	(false discovery rate $< 0.05$). Results surviving Bonferroni correction are
   190	marked $^*$; those surviving BH--FDR correction are marked $^\dag$.
   191	
   192	\paragraph{Power Analysis.}
   193	All inferential tests in this paper are evaluated against pre-specified power
   194	requirements using \texttt{statsmodels.stats.power.TTestIndPower} with
   195	$\alpha = 0.05$ and target power $1 - \beta = 0.80$.
   196	
   197	\textbf{Modal H100 experiments ($n = 5$ seeds per arm).}
   198	The minimum detectable effect size (MDE) at 80\% power for a two-sample
   199	Welch $t$-test with $n_1 = n_2 = 5$ is
   200	$d_{\min} = 2.024$ (Cohen's $d$).
   201	Table~\ref{tab:power-analysis} reports achieved power for a range of effect sizes.
   202	This threshold falls in the \emph{very large} range, meaning that
   203	comparisons yielding $|d| < 2.02$
   204	are not adequately powered to detect true differences.
   205	
   206	\textbf{Single-seed Tinker experiments ($n = 1$).}
   207	Single-seed runs have \emph{zero statistical power}: with only one observation per
   208	condition, no inferential test can be computed and no confidence intervals can be
   209	formed.  All Tinker single-run results are treated as \emph{descriptive only} and
   210	are not subject to significance testing.
   211	
   212	\textbf{TRL baseline ($n = 5$ seeds).}
   213	The TRL-GRPO baseline (Qwen2.5-0.5B, 5 seeds) achieves MDE
   214	$d_{\min} = 2.024$ for equal-$n$ comparisons.
   215	When compared to the pooled Classic-RL group ($n = 15$), the MDE
   216	improves to $d_{\min} = 1.530$,
   217	reflecting higher power from the larger reference group.
   218	
   219	\paragraph{Multiple-Comparison Correction.}
   220	We report 20 hypothesis tests across the paper.
   221	To control the false discovery rate (FDR), we apply the
   222	\textbf{Benjamini--Hochberg (BH)} procedure~\cite{benjamini1995controlling}
   223	at FDR $= 0.05$.  Under BH, the $i$-th ranked $p$-value (ordered ascending)
   224	is deemed significant when $p_{(i)} \le \frac{i}{m} \cdot 0.05$,
   225	where $m = 20$ is the total number of tests.
   226	Table~\ref{tab:bh-pvalues} lists all raw $p$-values alongside BH-adjusted
   227	$p$-values; 19 of 20 tests pass BH at FDR $=0.05$, but we caution against
   228	reading this as 19 independently replicated findings: several tests use
   229	per-step trajectory rewards that are autocorrelated (see the Mann--Whitney
   230	PPO/GRPO caveat in \S\ref{sec:ppo_grpo}), and correlations and ANOVAs
   231	over heterogeneous runs do not recover independent-replication evidence.
   232	BH controls FDR \emph{within} this pre-registered set of 20 tests on
   233	trace-level observations; it does not upgrade trace-level descriptive
   234	effect sizes into inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks.
   235	
   236	Note: These are trace-level descriptive effect sizes, not independent-run inferential statistics. Trace-level differences are descriptive, not inferential.  Effect sizes are reported as Cohen's $d$ for
   237	$t$-tests and Pearson/Spearman $r$ for correlations.  Bootstrap 95\%
   238	confidence intervals ($B = 10{,}000$) are reported for all means.
   239	
