   161	\section{Statistical Protocol}
   162	\label{app:stat_protocol}
   163	
   164	This appendix gives the full specification of the statistical rigor
   165	pass (Task~6) used to annotate every comparison in the paper. All
   166	numbers in Tables~\ref{tab:main_results_stats}--\ref{tab:ppo_grpo_stats}
   167	are produced by \texttt{experiments/compute\_statistics.py}
   168	and \texttt{experiments/render\_stat\_rigor\_tex.py} with
   169	\texttt{MASTER\_SEED}~$=~20260506$; rerunning the script on the
   170	committed artefacts produces byte-identical JSON.
   171	
   172	\subsection{Point Estimates and Bootstrap Confidence Intervals}
   173	For any sample $x = (x_1, \ldots, x_n)$, we report the empirical mean
   174	$\bar{x} = n^{-1} \sum_i x_i$ and a 95\,\% percentile bootstrap CI
   175	with $B = 10,000$ resamples:
   176	\[
   177	  \hat{\theta}^{(b)} = \bar{x}^{(b)}, \quad b = 1, \ldots, B, \qquad
   178	  \mathrm{CI}_{95\%} = \bigl[\,Q_{0.025}(\hat{\theta}^{(b)}),\; Q_{0.975}(\hat{\theta}^{(b)})\,\bigr].
   179	\]
   180	Resampling uses \texttt{np.random.Generator} seeded by a
   181	\texttt{SeedSequence}(MASTER\_SEED, spawn\_key=BLAKE2(tag)); each call
   182	site derives its own independent stream, so adding a new comparison does
   183	not perturb the numbers reported for existing ones. Paired bootstrap CIs
   184	for $\mu_A - \mu_B$ use independent draws of $A$ and $B$ because the
   185	reward traces are independent per-step samples from the two runs.
   186	
   187	\subsection{Effect Sizes}
   188	We report Cohen's $d$ with pooled standard deviation
   189	\[
   190	  d = \frac{\bar{x}_A - \bar{x}_B}{s_p},\qquad
   191	  s_p = \sqrt{\tfrac{(n_A-1)s_A^2 + (n_B-1)s_B^2}{n_A+n_B-2}},
   192	\]
   193	together with Hedges' $g = J\cdot d$ using the small-sample correction
   194	$J = 1 - 3 / (4 \mathrm{df} - 1)$, $\mathrm{df} = n_A + n_B - 2$.
   195	The 95\,\% CI uses the Hedges--Olkin (1985) analytical variance
   196	$\widehat{\mathrm{Var}}(d) = (n_A+n_B)/(n_A n_B) + d^2/\{2(n_A+n_B)\}$
   197	combined with $z_{0.975}=1.96$. Magnitude labels follow Cohen's
   198	convention (negligible $<0.2$; small $0.2$--$0.5$; medium $0.5$--$0.8$;
   199	large $0.8$--$1.2$; very large $\geq 1.2$). One-sample effects
   200	(Table~\ref{tab:results_gsm8k_stats}) use $d=(\bar{x}-\mu_0)/s$ with the
   201	one-sample variance $\mathrm{Var}(d) = 1/n + d^2/(2n)$.
   202	
   203	\subsection{Hypothesis Tests}
   204	The test chosen for each comparison matches its sampling structure:
   205	\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em]
   206	  \item \textbf{Table~\ref{tab:main_results_stats}} (late-vs-early within an experiment):
   207	    two-sided Mann--Whitney $U$ on the first-10 and last-10 reward samples
   208	    (robust to heavy-tailed step-level reward distributions).
   209	  \item \textbf{Table~\ref{tab:results_arithmetic_stats}} (cross-library):
   210	    Welch's two-sample $t$-test (unequal variances) against the TRL (GRPO)
   211	    reference.
   212	  \item \textbf{Table~\ref{tab:results_gsm8k_stats}} (post-RL vs.\ baseline):
   213	    one-sample $t$-test against the deterministic-eval baseline.
   214	  \item \textbf{Table~\ref{tab:ppo_grpo_stats}} (matched-model PPO vs.\ GRPO):
   215	    both Welch's $t$-test and Mann--Whitney $U$; the latter is reported as
   216	    a non-parametric robustness check.
   217	\end{itemize}
   218	
   219	\subsection{Multiple-Comparison Correction}
   220	The paper reports $k=38$ comparisons in total across Tables
   221	\ref{tab:main_results_stats}--\ref{tab:ppo_grpo_stats}. For every raw
   222	$p$-value $p_i$ we report
   223	\[
   224	  p_i^{\text{Bonf}} = \min(k \cdot p_i,\, 1),\qquad k = 38,
   225	\]
   226	as the headline correction (family-wise error rate $\leq 0.05$).
   227	Benjamini--Hochberg FDR-adjusted $p$-values at $q=0.05$ are also
   228	reported in \texttt{experiments/statistical\_analysis.json} as a less
   229	conservative alternative. Stars in every table reflect the
   230	\emph{Bonferroni} decision, not the BH decision.
   231	
   232	\subsection{Determinism}
   233	Every random draw in the pipeline is routed through a single
   234	\texttt{SeedSequence(MASTER\_SEED)} whose \texttt{spawn\_key} is the
   235	BLAKE2 digest of a human-readable tag (e.g.\ \texttt{t4\_diff:Qwen3-8B}).
   236	Because BLAKE2 is stable across Python processes (unlike the built-in
   237	\texttt{hash()}), two runs of the pipeline on the same committed
   238	artefacts produce byte-identical \texttt{statistical\_analysis.json} and
   239	\texttt{stat\_rigor\_tables.json}. We verify this in CI by running the
   240	script twice and comparing MD5 checksums.
   241	
   242	\subsection{Family-Wide \texorpdfstring{$p$}{p}-Value Summary}
   243	Table~\ref{tab:family_wide_stats} lists every comparison contributing to
   244	the $k$-test family used for Bonferroni correction.
