     9	\section{Ethics, Limitations, and Broader Impact}
    10	\label{sec:impact_standalone}
    11	
    12	This statement consolidates the dual-use analysis, itemised compute
    13	accounting, carbon footprint estimation, data provenance disclosures, a
    14	candid acknowledgment of the closed-source training backend on which a
    15	fraction of our headline numbers depends, and a list of methodological
    16	limits we are aware of but did not have resources to close within the
    17	submission window.  It complements the shorter Limitations and Broader
    18	Impact subsections embedded in Section~\ref{sec:limitations}.
    19	
    20	% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    21	\subsection{Dual-Use Analysis: Misuse Risks of Reasoning RL}
    22	\label{sec:dualuse}
    23	% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    24	
    25	\paragraph{Threat model.}
    26	\ours{} releases (i) training scripts that apply GRPO to the GSM8K
    27	\cite{cobbe2021training} mathematical reasoning benchmark and to the
    28	Salesforce xLAM-Function-Calling-60k \cite{liu2024xlam} tool-use corpus,
    29	(ii) a set of LoRA adapters published on the HuggingFace Hub
    30	(\texttt{arvindcr4/tinker-rl-bench-*}), and (iii) diagnostic code for
    31	Zero-Variance Fraction and reward-instability analysis. We
    32	analyse four concrete misuse pathways these artefacts could, in principle,
    33	enable, and we describe the existing guardrails and the residual risk we
    34	judge acceptable for publication.
    35	
    36	\paragraph{M1. Weaponisation of mathematical reasoning.}
    37	The most capability-relevant artefact we release is code for running
    38	GRPO-style verifier-reward experiments on grade-school arithmetic and
    39	multi-step prompt suites. In our clean held-out GSM8K control, the measured
    40	gain is small and not statistically significant, so we do not claim the code
    41	improves general reasoning capability. A malicious
    42	operator could attempt to extend this pipeline to tasks of greater
    43	dual-use concern, e.g., chemistry olympiad problems, cryptographic key
    44	recovery, or financial fraud. We judge the \emph{marginal} uplift provided
    45	by our release to be small for three reasons. First, GRPO at our training
    46	scale does not create new capabilities; the base-model control for GSM8K
    47	(Section~\ref{sec:results}, 82.0\%) shows that the bulk of held-out
    48	accuracy is attributable to Qwen3-8B's pretraining. Our full GRPO run only
    49	adds \(+1.3\) percentage points ($p{=}0.26$, not statistically significant).
    50	Second, the public literature already contains GRPO implementations
    51	\cite{shao2024deepseekmath, openrlhf2024, verl2024, trl2020} that are at
    52	least as capable. Third, any reward-harness improvement is bounded by the
    53	rewarder: GSM8K rewards use exact-match against a human-written gold answer
    54	and do not transfer to the domains listed above without a new validated reward
    55	signal, which itself requires expertise and labelled data.
    56	
    57	\paragraph{M2. Reward hacking and the long-term alignment risk.}
    58	GRPO, like all policy-gradient RL from a proxy reward, is vulnerable to
    59	reward hacking: the model learns to exploit idiosyncrasies of the reward
    60	function rather than solve the intended task \cite{skalse2022specificationgaming,
    61	krakovna2020specification}. Our reward-instability audit
    62	(Section~\ref{sec:length_bias}) flags verbosity-trap-like regressions in 4/11
    63	GRPO trajectories, but treats them as proxy-reward failures rather than proof
    64	that response length alone caused the regression.
    65	Users who apply our scripts to under-specified reward functions in
    66	production settings (e.g., a custom ``helpfulness'' classifier) may observe
    67	models that appear to improve on held-out metrics while silently
    68	optimising against unintended proxies. We include the Zero-Variance
    69	Fraction and reward-stability diagnostics precisely so that downstream
    70	users can detect such drift.
    71	
    72	\paragraph{M3. Circumventing safety training via distillation or tool-use.}
    73	The tool-use track trains LoRA adapters that teach a base model to emit
    74	schema-valid JSON function calls \cite{liu2024xlam}. A motivated adversary
    75	could in principle use the same pipeline to teach a base model to emit
    76	jailbreak prompts as ``tool calls'' or to invoke an adversarial external
    77	tool. We mitigate this by (a) releasing only adapters, not merged weights,
    78	so that the safety-trained instruct checkpoint must be applied separately
    79	and any instruct-layer guardrails remain active; (b) documenting in model
    80	cards that the tool-use adapters are trained on a narrow schema (five
    81	synthetic tools) and have not been safety-evaluated for open-ended function
    82	calling; and (c) not releasing any function-calling harness that would
    83	actually execute emitted calls.
    84	
    85	\paragraph{M4. Compute-efficiency externalities.}
    86	Our work argues that critic-free RL with careful group-size selection
    87	(\(G{=}32\)) can be run on modest budgets. Improved training efficiency is
    88	itself dual-use: it lowers the cost of running adversarial fine-tuning at
    89	scale. We judge this externality to be modest relative to the already-low
    90	cost of fine-tuning a 0.6B--8B model with commodity LoRA recipes
    91	\cite{hu2022lora}, but we flag it explicitly.
    92	
    93	\paragraph{Residual risk.}
    94	After weighing M1--M4 we judge that the reproducibility, calibration, and
    95	pedagogical benefits of releasing \ours{} outweigh the residual misuse
    96	risk. We adopt the standard mitigation of releasing adapters (not merged
    97	weights), documenting intended use and out-of-scope use in model cards,
    98	and publishing alongside the diagnostics a researcher would need to detect
    99	reward hacking.
   100	
