   478	\subsection{Task~4: Stack-Sensitivity Probe Under a Nominal GRPO Config}
   479	\label{sec:framework_gap}
   480	
   481	We attempted a nominal framework-swap comparison under matched visible
   482	hyperparameters (Qwen3-8B, seed~42, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,
   483	GSM8K-500, 30 steps).  This is not evidence that one framework causally
   484	dominates another.  Backend-level implementation details---checkpoint and
   485	tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss masking,
   486	KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules, precision,
   487	rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and evaluator details---remain part of
   488	the treatment.  The comparison is therefore interpreted as stack-sensitivity
   489	evidence: visible GRPO hyperparameters alone do not fully specify the training
   490	process.  In the current artifact, only the Tinker and TRL rows are real runs;
   491	the verl and OpenRLHF rows are deterministic dry-run fallbacks and support
   492	engineering-readiness discussion only.  Results are serialised to
   493	\texttt{experiments/results/framework\_comparison.json} and visualised as a
   494	four-bar last-10-reward chart (Figure~\ref{fig:framework_gap}).
   495	
   496	\begin{figure}[htbp]
   497	\centering
   498	\includegraphics[width=0.8\columnwidth]{figures/v2/framework_comparison.pdf}
   499	\caption{Task~4 stack-sensitivity probe.  The visible GRPO configuration is
   500	nominally matched (Qwen3-8B, $G{=}8$, lr${=}10^{-5}$, GSM8K-500, 30 steps,
   501	seed~42), but the full post-training stack is not identical.  Tinker and TRL
   502	are real runs; verl and OpenRLHF are dry-run fallbacks in the current artifact.
   503	The figure is evidence that ``GRPO config'' is under-specified unless backend,
   504	sampler, loss-mask, KL/reference, LoRA, precision, checkpoint, and evaluator
   505	details are reported, not a causal framework ranking.}
   506	\label{fig:framework_gap}
   507	\end{figure}
   508	
