   580	
   581	\paragraph{Post-selection warning.}
   582	The ten checkpoints in Table~\ref{tab:heldout_gsm8k} below are chosen by
   583	ranking all $\sim$70 campaign runs by training \emph{last-10} mean reward and
   584	taking the top ten.  This is a checkpoint-selection analysis, not a
   585	single-model held-out generalization result.  It is not comparable to, and
   586	does not contradict, the abstract's clean paired control on Qwen3-8B, where
   587	held-out GSM8K improves only from $82.0\%$ to $83.3\%$ ($p=0.26, paired per-prompt p=0.539 (evaluated on a random 50-problem subset)$).  Reading
   588	this table as ``GRPO lifts GSM8K to 87--95\%'' would be the selection-induced
   589	overclaim that the abstract explicitly warns against.
   590	
   591	\subsection{Held-out Capability: GSM8K Generalization}
   592	\paragraph{Held-out GSM8K evaluation of the top-10 checkpoints.}
   593	To separate optimisation from generalisation we selected the ten Tinker
   594	checkpoints with the highest training \emph{last-10} mean reward across our
   595	campaign (\S\ref{sec:main_results}) and re-evaluated every checkpoint on a
   596	deterministic, held-out slice of $N=500$ problems drawn from the GSM8K
   597	\texttt{test} split --- strictly disjoint from every training batch
   598	(\texttt{seed}$=0$). Decoding is greedy ($T{=}0$, \texttt{max\_tokens}
   599	$=512$) and we score with the same boxed-answer reward used during training,
   600	so held-out accuracy is directly comparable to \emph{last-10}.
   601	The evaluator and the raw per-problem trace are released at
   602	\texttt{experiments/modal/modal\_heldout\_eval.py} and
   603	\texttt{experiments/results/heldout\_gsm8k.json}.
