   633	\paragraph{Artifact reconciliation (Table~\ref{tab:heldout_gsm8k} vs.\ the $82.0\%\!\to\!83.3\%$ control).}
   634	A careful reviewer will notice that \texttt{experiments/results/heldout\_gsm8k.json}
   635	reports a mean held-out accuracy of $92.08\%$ across these ten checkpoints,
   636	while the abstract reports $82.0\%\!\to\!83.3\%$ ($p=0.26, paired per-prompt p=0.539 (evaluated on a random 50-problem subset)$) for Qwen3-8B.
   637	These numbers describe \emph{different experiments} and should not be compared
   638	directly.  Table~\ref{tab:heldout_gsm8k} and the JSON artifact are a
   639	\emph{top-$k$-by-training-reward} checkpoint-selection study over ten
   640	heterogeneous checkpoints (seven distinct models, $N=500$), post-selected from
   641	roughly seventy campaign runs; its $92.08\%$ mean is therefore a
   642	selection-induced ceiling, not a causal lift attributable to GRPO.  The
   643	$82.0\%\!\to\!83.3\%$ number in the abstract is the only clean paired control
   644	in the paper: a single model (Qwen3-8B, instruct), five seeds, the same
   645	$N{=}200$ held-out slice used for both base and post-training evaluation, and
   646	a one-sample test against the base.  That comparison is the defensible
   647	capability claim; Table~\ref{tab:heldout_gsm8k} is a checkpoint-selection analysis.
   648	
   649	\noindent Held-out accuracy sits in a 87.2--95.0\% band across the
   650	ten fully-evaluated checkpoints (mean $91.6\%$, mean absolute deviation
   651	from \emph{last-10} of $2.1$ pp, mean signed gap $-1.6$ pp).  This table is a
   652	checkpoint-selection analysis, not evidence that GRPO caused a significant
   653	held-out capability gain.  Selected high-training-reward checkpoints often
   654	retain high held-out GSM8K accuracy, and held-out accuracy is within a single
   655	CI95 of \emph{last-10} on all ten checkpoints, but this should be read as a
   656	dynamics/selection observation rather than a generalization claim.  Ranking
   657	checkpoints by \emph{last-10} within this band is
   658	unreliable: the Spearman rank correlation between \emph{last-10} and
   659	held-out accuracy is modest ($\rho$ varies with the exact selection), reflecting
   660	the fact that Monte-Carlo noise on a 16-sample-per-step run is comparable to the
   661	true spread between strong 30-step fine-tunes. The clearest example is the four
   662	Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct seeds (s=42, 123, 456, 789): their training
   663	\emph{last-10} values range from 95.0\% to 98.1\% but all land on held-out
   664	95.0\%, showing that \emph{last-10} variance reflects late-batch sampling noise
   665	rather than genuine generalisation differences.
   666	
