  1433	% =============================================================================
  1434	\subsection{Campaign Extension: Additional Runs Under the Training-Reward Parser}
  1435	\label{sec:bitter_lesson}
  1436	% =============================================================================
  1437	
  1438	We launched a systematic extension of 32 additional Tinker GRPO experiments
  1439	plus 3 TRL-GRPO stack-comparison experiments on Modal H100s, bringing our
  1440	total to 70+ training runs.  These runs are \emph{not} a test of the ``bitter
  1441	lesson'' hypothesis: they measure training-time reward under our existing
  1442	parsers, not held-out benchmark ceiling.  We keep them here for the ZVF/GU diagnostic
  1443	coverage they provide.
  1444	
  1445	\paragraph{New Larger Models (training-reward parser, not held-out benchmark).}
  1446	We evaluated six previously untested models on GSM8K with GRPO (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, Campaign Extension block).
  1447	\textbf{Kimi-K2-Thinking} had the highest sustained online reward in this block
  1448	(peak 100\%, last-10 95.8\%), suggesting that models already specialized for
  1449	reasoning may be better matched to this verifier-reward setup.
  1450	\textbf{Qwen3.5-397B-A17B}---the largest MoE model in our study at 397B total /
  1451	17B active---reached 100\% peak with 97.9\% last-10 average under
  1452	the training parser; this is an online-reward ceiling in the recorded campaign,
  1453	not a held-out benchmark ceiling. \textbf{GPT-OSS-120B} showed stable online
  1454	reward (93.8\% at step 1, 87.5\% last-10), consistent with reinforcement of an
  1455	already strong GSM8K prior rather than newly established capability. Conversely,
  1456	\textbf{DeepSeek-V3.1-Base} (671B/37B active) achieved only 57.3\% last-10
  1457	despite its scale, reinforcing that parameter count alone does not specify the
  1458	effective starting policy.
  1459	
  1460	\paragraph{Stack Sensitivity, Not Framework Ranking.}
  1461	The TRL-GRPO experiments on Modal H100 expose a reporting problem rather than
  1462	a clean causal framework effect.  Under nominally matched visible
  1463	hyperparameters on Qwen3-8B, the Tinker row reaches 84.4\% last-10 reward while the TRL row reaches
  1464	5.0\% (peak 37.5\%).  This large divergence does not prove that Tinker is
  1465	better than TRL, because framework, backend defaults, sampler behavior,
  1466	loss masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA targets, precision/backend,
  1467	and evaluator details remain entangled.  It proves the more defensible point:
  1468	the phrase ``same GRPO config'' is under-specified unless those hidden stack
  1469	variables are logged and controlled.  The Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct row performs
  1470	substantially better (71.3\% last-10), reinforcing the stack-conditioned
  1471	interpretation rather than a universal framework ranking.
  1472	
  1473	\paragraph{Base vs.~Instruct Models.}
  1474	A clear pattern emerges: base models (Llama-3.1-70B at 36.4\%, Llama-3.1-8B at 11.5\%, DeepSeek-V3.1-Base at 57.3\%) struggle substantially compared to instruct counterparts (qualitative, no statistical test).
  1475	This aligns with the GRPO-as-DPO interpretation~\cite{wu2025grpo_dpo}: GRPO requires models that already produce \emph{some} correct completions to generate meaningful preference pairs.
  1476	Base models lacking instruction-following capability produce uniformly low-quality completions, yielding zero-variance groups that prevent learning.
  1477	
  1478	\paragraph{Group Size Ablation.}
  1479	We swept group size $G \in \{2, 4, 8, 16\}$ on Qwen3-8B (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, Group Size block).
  1480	The highest observed last-10 reward in this single-seed 30-step sweep occurs at
  1481	$G{=}8$ (84.4\%), with $G{=}4$ second (52.1\%) and both $G{=}2$ (37.5\%) and
  1482	$G{=}16$ (38.0\%) lower. This inverted-U pattern is consistent with a finite-group
  1483	tradeoff: too few samples can miss reward diversity, while larger groups can
  1484	increase rollout cost and change the effective update distribution. A completed
  1485	multi-seed sweep would be needed before calling $G{=}8$ optimal.
  1486	
