     4	\section{Introduction}
     5	\label{sec:intro}
     6	
     7	PPO~\citep{schulman2017proximal}, GRPO~\citep{shao2024deepseekmath}, and
     8	DPO~\citep{rafailov2024direct} are frequently contrasted as though the
     9	algorithm label captures the full experimental treatment.  In LLM
    10	post-training, outcomes also
    11	depend on base model, instruction tuning, reward parser, group construction,
    12	sampler, backend, LoRA configuration, checkpoint selection, and evaluation
    13	harness.  This is the LLM post-training analogue of the reproducibility
    14	concerns Henderson et al. identified for deep RL~\citep{henderson2018deep}:
    15	algorithm labels are under-specified unless the full training stack is fixed
    16	or modeled.
    17	
    18	This paper audits a group-relative RL runner inspired by GRPO, not a
    19	canonical GRPO reproduction.  It retains group-normalized advantages and a
    20	critic-free structure, but does not implement the PPO ratio and clip of
    21	\citet{schulman2017proximal}, does not retain a frozen reference policy for
    22	KL regularization, performs one optimizer step per rollout batch, and does
    23	not use a completion-only token mask.  A P1-A diagnostic on Qwen3.5-4B
    24	measures 61.6--89.6\% of the full-sequence loss magnitude from prompt tokens
    25	rather than completion tokens.  Claims in this paper concern this runner,
    26	not canonical GRPO.
    27	
    28	The paper does not show that this runner broadly improves reasoning.  The
    29	only clean held-out capability result is a paired GSM8K 200-example evaluation
    30	that improves only $82.0\% \to 83.3\%$ ($p = 0.26$); we do not claim a
    31	significant held-out math effect.  The paper's positive contribution is a
    32	diagnostic protocol: how to decide whether a specific model--reward--sampler--
    33	stack combination has usable group-relative RL signal, and how to scope
    34	claims accordingly.
    35	
    36	\begin{table}[t]
    37	\centering
    38	\caption{Claim--evidence--verdict hierarchy.}
    39	\label{tab:claim_evidence_verdict}
    40	\small
    41	\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.27\linewidth}p{0.18\linewidth}p{0.33\linewidth}p{0.16\linewidth}@{}}
    42	\toprule
    43	\textbf{Claim} & \textbf{Status} & \textbf{Headline evidence} & \textbf{Verdict}\\
    44	\midrule
    45	C1: The runner is a reward-contrast amplifier. &
    46	Mechanistic + operational &
    47	Mixed-group probability formula; ZVF/GU triage (collapse/saturation/contrast). &
    48	Signal exists only when sampled groups contain reward diversity.\\
    49	C2: ZVF/GU provide early triage diagnostics. &
    50	Most operational contribution &
    51	First-5-step rule (ZVF $\geq 80\%$, reward $\leq 5\%$) catches all collapsed runs
    52	in a 22-run validation set containing only 2 positive collapse cases
    53	(precision 1.0 and recall 1.0 are therefore power-bounded, not a measure of
    54	predictive strength). ZVF is task-driven. &
    55	Triage, not prediction. Not a calibrated performance model.\\
    56	C3: Training reward conflates with held-out capability. &
    57	Central negative result &
    58	Held-out GSM8K $82.0\% \to 83.3\%$, $p = 0.26$; checkpoint-selection over-optimism. &
    59	Training reward supports dynamics claims only.\\
    60	C4: Algorithm labels (PPO, GRPO, DPO) are under-specified. &
    61	Methodological warning &
    62	17$\times$ last-10 gap under nominally matched configs; artifact sensitivity. &
    63	Full-stack reporting required.\\
    64	\bottomrule
    65	\end{tabular}
    66	\end{table}
    67	
    68	\subsection{Claim 1: Reward-contrast amplifier mechanism.}
    69	A group-relative update has useful signal only when the sampled group contains
    70	distinguishable reward outcomes.  Under binary or verifier-like rewards, if
    71	the current policy succeeds on prompt $x$ with probability $p_x$ and group size
    72	is $G$, the probability of a mixed, gradient-carrying group is
    73	\[
    74	P(\text{usable}) \approx \frac{1}{N}\sum_x \left[1 - (1-p_x)^G - p_x^G\right].
    75	\]
    76	The familiar $p_x > 1/G$ rule is only an expected-one-success heuristic; it
    77	is not a threshold theorem.  This mechanism explains why warm-starts, group
    78	size, reward density, and task difficulty matter more than task labels.
    79	When ZVF $= 1$ (all groups identical-reward), no gradient flows; when ZVF $= 0$
    80	(all groups mixed-reward), the update exploits within-group contrast.
    81	
    82	\subsection{Claim 2: ZVF/GU as triage diagnostics.}
    83	Zero-Variance Fraction (ZVF) measures the fraction of prompts where all $G$
    84	completions receive identical rewards, producing zero gradient.
    85	Gradient Utilization (GU) $= 1 - $ZVF measures the fraction of prompts
    86	providing usable signal.  High ZVF with low reward indicates cold-start
    87	collapse; high ZVF with high reward indicates saturation; low ZVF indicates
    88	within-group contrast.  A fixed first-five-step rule (ZVF $\geq 80\%$, reward
    89	$\leq 5\%$) catches both collapsed runs in a 22-run de-duplicated validation
    90	set with no false positives, but only two positive failures are observed and
    91	continuous ZVF ranking is weak.  The defensible claim is triage, not
    92	prediction: ZVF/GU can support early decisions to stop, warm-start, densify
    93	reward, adjust task difficulty, or change group size.  Importantly, ZVF is
    94	task-driven, not model-scale-driven: tool-use experiments saturate at ZVF $= 100\%$
    95	regardless of model size, while GSM8K experiments average ZVF $= 8.5\%$.
    96	
    97	The mixed-group probability formula is the explanatory core; ZVF/GU is the
    98	operational interface.
    99	
   100	\subsection{Claim 3: Training reward is not capability.}
   101	Online reward trajectories describe the reward environment that produced the
   102	update, so a distinct held-out evaluation is required before upgrading them to
   103	reasoning or tool-use claims~\citep{lambert2024rewardbench,dubois2024lengthcontrolled}.
   104	The clean GSM8K control improves only
   105	$82.0\% \to 83.3\%$ ($p = 0.26$), so we do not claim a significant held-out
   106	math effect.  Held-out evaluation of the top-10 checkpoints (selected by
   107	training last-10 reward) yields an 87.2--95.0\% accuracy band, but this is a
   108	checkpoint-selection analysis: selecting by training reward introduces
   109	over-optimism.  Four Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct seeds range 95.0--98.1\% training
   110	last-10 but all land on 95.0\% held-out accuracy, showing that last-10
   111	variance reflects Monte-Carlo sampling noise rather than genuine
   112	generalization differences.
   113	
   114	Tool-use, HumanEval, and MATH rows remain lower-tier evidence: tool-use
   115	scores schema compliance (not executed success), the HumanEval sandbox
   116	removes Python built-ins, and some prompt pools come from test splits used as
   117	reward-environment probes.  These are useful as dynamics evidence and as sparse-
   118	reward failure cases, not as proof of agentic capability.
   119	
   120	\subsection{Claim 4: Algorithm labels are under-specified treatments.}
   121	Algorithm-level claims require full-stack specification.  Under nominally
   122	matched visible configurations (Qwen3-8B, $G=8$, lr$=10^{-5}$, GSM8K, 30
   123	steps, seed 42), the Tinker runner reaches 84.4\% last-10 reward while TRL
   124	on Modal H100 reaches 5.0\%.  This 17$\times$ gap does not prove Tinker is
   125	better than TRL; it proves that ``same GRPO config'' is under-specified unless
   126	backend, sampler, reward parser, LoRA configuration, optimizer,
   127	checkpoint-selection rule, and evaluator are reported and controlled.  Our
   128	Qwen PPO/GRPO comparison is artifact-sensitive (PPO last-10 differs between
   129	ledger and statistical summary), and the Llama comparison favors PPO but is
   130	single-seed and backend-confounded.  No universal PPO-versus-GRPO ranking
   131	is supported.
   132	
   133	\subsection{Contributions.}
   134	This paper contributes:
   135	(i)~a mixed-group probability mechanism explaining when a group-relative
   136	RL loop has learning signal;
   137	(ii)~ZVF/GU as early triage diagnostics separating cold-start collapse,
   138	saturation, and usable contrast;
   139	(iii)~a held-out GSM8K result showing that training reward conflates with
   140	capability ($82.0\% \to 83.3\%$, $p = 0.26$);
   141	(iv)~a methodological warning that PPO, GRPO, and DPO comparisons require
   142	full-stack specification;
   143	(v)~a diagnostic protocol for deciding whether a specific model--reward--
   144	sampler--stack combination has usable group-relative RL signal.
   145	We release the code, run logs, and checkpoints at
